### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The fact-check reveals that the cited figures of 558 people killed by police and 201 deaths in police custody in 2018 originate from entirely separate countries and reporting authorities, with distinct definitions. The number 558 pertains to "deaths as a result of police action" in South Africa, as reported by the [Independent Police Investigative Directorate (IPID)](http://apcof.org/wp-content/uploads/025-aresouthafricascopsaccountable-resultsofindependentpoliceinvestigativedirectorateinvestigations-davidbruce.pdf), while 201 refers to deaths "in or after detention" in the United Kingdom, reported by the [Independent Office for Police Conduct (IOPC)](https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2018/jul/25/police-custody-deaths-hit-10-year-high-with-experts-citing-austerity). The claim misleadingly presents these numbers as a single correlated set from one context, which is refuted by the evidence showing they are not from the same dataset or jurisdiction.